<% content_for :title, "Cravings | Bipolar Sober" %>

<main class="addiction-triggers-page addiction-cravings-page">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section
    class="addiction-triggers-hero"
    style="
      --triggers-mobile: url('<%= asset_path("cravings_mobile_background.png") %>');
      --triggers-desktop: url('<%= asset_path("cravings_desktop_background.png") %>');
    "
  >
    <div class="addiction-triggers-hero-content">

      <p class="addiction-triggers-eyebrow">
        BIPOLAR SOBER / ADDICTION / CRAVINGS
      </p>

      <h1>
        <span>ON</span>
        <span>CRAVINGS.</span>
      </h1>

      <p class="addiction-triggers-intro">
        THE WANTING. THE BARGAINING. THE FUCK-ITS.<br>
        YOU CAN WANT IT AND STILL LEAVE IT ALONE.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 01 / THE WANTING -->
  <section class="addiction-triggers-section at-pink">

    <div class="at-heading">
      <p class="at-number">01 / THE WANTING</p>

      <h2>
        DON’T<br>
        TEMPT ME.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="at-copy">

      <blockquote>
        “Don’t tempt me, Frodo.”
        <br><br>
        — Gandalf
      </blockquote>

      <p class="at-lead">
        You can know exactly why you stopped drinking
        and still want some fucking whiskey.
      </p>

      <p>
        You remember what happened, the consequences,
        and the sober life you are building.
        Your imagination still offers the first sip,
        the familiar feeling, and a mental vacation
        from whatever is happening.
      </p>

      <p>
        The trigger might be a backache, a boring evening,
        some bipolar bullshit, or a beer commercial.
        The craving is what happens afterward:
        you start focusing on drinking.
      </p>

      <p>
        You imagine it. You consider it.
        A fleeting thought starts living rent free
        upstairs in your brain.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 02 / SELECTIVE MEMORY -->
  <section class="addiction-triggers-section at-yellow">

    <div class="at-heading">
      <p class="at-number">02 / SELECTIVE MEMORY</p>

      <h2>
        REMEMBER<br>
        THE WHOLE<br>
        FUCKING<br>
        STORY.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="at-copy">

      <p class="at-lead">
        The anticipation gives you a preview
        of the evening you want.
      </p>

      <p>
        You imagine relief before anything has happened.
        Your mind paints the good times and leaves out
        the impaired decisions and the consequences
        you will have to manage afterward.
      </p>

      <p>
        Bring your own history into the picture.
        What happened when you made similar promises?
        What do you tend to overlook once the desire
        gets strong?
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        INCLUDE WHAT HAPPENS<br>
        AFTER THE FIRST DRINK.
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        You do not need to frighten or humiliate yourself.
        Give the decision enough information to include
        more than the relief you hope drinking will provide.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 03 / BARGAINING -->
  <section class="addiction-triggers-section at-purple">

    <div class="at-heading">
      <p class="at-number">03 / THE NEGOTIATION</p>

      <h2>
        JUST ONE.<br>
        JUST TONIGHT.<br>
        JUST THIS<br>
        ONCE.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="at-copy">

      <p>
        You have been doing well. You deserve a break.
        You will deal with tomorrow when it gets here.
        Each thought makes a little more room
        for the outcome you want.
      </p>

      <p>
        Notice when you start looking for permission
        instead of considering the decision honestly.
        What are you promising yourself,
        and what makes that promise reliable?
      </p>

      <p>
        The urge may come with a real need:
        relief from pain, some enjoyment,
        a break from your thoughts.
        That need deserves attention while you question
        whether drinking will help you meet it.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        WHAT DO YOU WANT?<br>
        WHAT ARE YOU EXPECTING IT TO GIVE YOU?
      </blockquote>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 04 / THE FUCK-ITS -->
  <section class="addiction-triggers-section at-black">

    <div class="at-heading">
      <p class="at-number">04 / WHEN YOU STOP CARING</p>

      <h2>
        THE<br>
        FUCK-ITS.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="at-copy">

      <p class="at-lead">
        Sometimes you stop arguing for drinking
        and stop caring about the argument altogether.
      </p>

      <p>
        You are tired of thinking.
        You want to act.
        Make the next step concrete:
        contact someone, open your drinking-deterrent page,
        or follow the first step of your relapse plan.
      </p>

      <p>
        You can still want the drink while putting distance
        between yourself and the opportunity to get it.
        You can feel annoyed about asking for help
        and ask anyway.
      </p>

      <p>
        Your feelings do not all have to agree
        before you do something that protects
        your recovery.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 05 / COMPETING NEEDS -->
  <section class="addiction-triggers-section at-pink">

    <div class="at-heading">
      <p class="at-number">05 / GIVE EVERY NEED A VOICE</p>

      <h2>
        PART OF YOU<br>
        WANTS IT.<br>
        WHAT ELSE<br>
        MATTERS?
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="at-copy">

      <p>
        A part of you may genuinely believe drinking will help.
        It remembers a familiar escape from pain,
        boredom, or an exhausting mental state.
        Listen to what that part is asking for.
      </p>

      <p>
        It may be desperate for comfort or furious
        about how much effort ordinary life requires.
        You can understand its intention while considering
        whether its proposed solution serves the rest of you.
      </p>

      <p>
        Other parts want stability, sobriety,
        enough money, and mornings without another
        mess to untangle. Give those needs a voice
        while the craving is loud.
      </p>

      <p>
        Think of something specific you want to protect:
        being present for Banjo, working on your website,
        finishing a creative project, or following through
        on tomorrow’s plans.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        ORDINARY THINGS STILL MATTER<br>
        WHEN IMMEDIATE RELIEF FEELS LOUDER.
      </blockquote>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 06 / URGENCY -->
  <section class="addiction-triggers-section at-yellow">

    <div class="at-heading">
      <p class="at-number">06 / THE DEMAND FOR RELIEF</p>

      <h2>
        IT HAS TO<br>
        STOP.<br>
        RIGHT NOW.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="at-copy">

      <p>
        Your pain is real. Managing your moods
        can be exhausting. You have reasons
        to want relief quickly.
      </p>

      <p>
        Still, urgency can narrow your attention
        until one option seems like the only option.
        Ask what support could help you through
        the next stretch, even if it cannot make
        everything better immediately.
      </p>

      <p>
        Treat that stretch as a practical challenge.
        What resources are available tonight?
        You might need company, food, distraction,
        help with a task, or attention to pain
        your current plan is not adequately addressing.
      </p>

      <p class="at-lead">
        You do not have to manufacture an amazing
        sober evening to justify staying sober
        through an ordinary difficult one.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 07 / SUPPORT -->
  <section class="addiction-triggers-section at-purple">

    <div class="at-heading">
      <p class="at-number">07 / SAY IT OUT LOUD</p>

      <h2>
        LET SOMEONE<br>
        KNOW BEFORE<br>
        YOU ACT.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="at-copy">

      <blockquote>
        “I WANT TO DRINK,<br>
        AND I’M STARTING TO MAKE EXCUSES FOR IT.”
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        A clear description gives someone a chance
        to respond before you have already made
        arrangements to use.
      </p>

      <p>
        Notice how the conversation affects the craving.
        Talking honestly can help you feel understood.
        An hour spent reminiscing about drinking
        may leave you wanting it more.
      </p>

      <p>
        Are you seeking support or finding someone
        to help romanticize the thing you are trying
        not to do? Redirect the conversation toward
        what would help you tonight.
      </p>

      <p>
        If the urge returns, return to your response.
        Read the page again. Contact someone again.
        Reassess what you need.
        Repetition does not make the effort meaningless.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 08 / COMPASSION -->
  <section class="addiction-triggers-section at-black">

    <div class="at-heading">
      <p class="at-number">08 / LEAVE SHAME OUT OF IT</p>

      <h2>
        A CRAVING<br>
        DOESN’T ERASE<br>
        YOUR WORK.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="at-copy">

      <p>
        Wanting a drink does not erase your sober days
        or the changes you have made.
        It gives you a present need to respond to.
      </p>

      <p>
        If it comes with concerning changes in mood,
        sleep, or impulsivity, include your treatment team
        rather than expecting your recovery tools
        to carry everything.
      </p>

      <p>
        Be honest about the appeal.
        Remember the full experience.
        Let support matter while you decide
        what comes next.
      </p>

      <p>
        You deserve relief and care throughout that process,
        including on the days when staying sober
        takes more effort than you wish it did.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- ENDING -->
  <section class="addiction-triggers-ending">

    <div>

      <p class="at-ending-small">
        MAKE ROOM FOR YOUR NEXT CHOICE.
      </p>

      <h2>
        YOU CAN<br>
        WANT IT.<br>
        <span>AND LEAVE<br>
        IT ALONE.</span>
      </h2>

    </div>

    <div class="at-ending-copy">

      <p>
        You can miss something and continue choosing
        to leave it behind.
      </p>

      <p>
        Give yourself time, use the plans you prepared,
        and bring support closer when you need it.
      </p>

      <p>
        The craving deserves attention.
        Your recovery deserves protection.
      </p>

      <%= link_to "← BACK TO ADDICTION",
          addiction_path,
          class: "at-back-button" %>

    </div>

  </section>

</main>